other than a normal single barline, it should be represented by a barline type that describes","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"it. This includes information about repeats and multiple endings, as well as line style. Barline data is on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the same level as the other musical data in a score — a child of a measure in a partwise score, or a part in a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"timewise score. This allows for barlines within measures, as in dotted barlines that subdivide measures in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"complex meters. The two fermata elements allow for fermatas on both sides of the barline (the lower one","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"inverted).","type":"text"}],"title":"MXLBarline","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLBarline","role":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlbarline","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"MXLBarline"}],"type":"topic"}}}